From a9ecb010ec24367a57fe70f11827e3e548ed860e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:59:44 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 14/28] GE: stencil test and operations, kept in step with the
 alpha bits

The stencil registers (0x24 enable, 0xDC function/reference/mask, 0xDD
operations) were ignored and the depth attachment had no stencil. A game
draws stencil shadow volumes (stencil-only passes, then passes that test
the stencil) and masks its characters out of a screen composite with the
stencil; all of it drew as if every test passed.

The PSP keeps the stencil in the frame buffer's alpha bits (1 in 5551,
4 in 4444, 8 in 8888, none in 5650). The renderer now keeps it twice: in
a stencil aspect of the depth attachment (D32_SFLOAT_S8_UINT or
D24_UNORM_S8_UINT), as the alpha bits widened to 8 bits, and in the
colour target's alpha. The stencil state goes into the pipeline key.
A clear that writes alpha writes the stencil from the clear colour; the
pixel mask limits stencil writes as it limits alpha writes. A draw with
the stencil test on leaves the alpha channel alone, since the GE puts the
stencil result there, not the draw's alpha.

Each target tracks which copy is newer. Before anything reads alpha (a
destination-alpha blend, a draw that writes alpha, a copy for sampling
the target, the write-back) the alpha is rebuilt from the stencil: alpha
cleared, then one stencil-tested additive quad per bit plane. Before a
draw tests or writes the stencil after alpha was written, the stencil is
rebuilt from a copy of the colour: stencil cleared, then one quad per bit
plane, with ge.frag's alpha test given a mask. A full-screen clear of
both leaves them in step. The perf line counts these copies ("stencil").
<prefix>_TRACE_STENCIL logs each; <prefix>_NO_STENCIL keeps the old
depth-only format and ignores the stencil.

Pipeline keys saved by earlier runs (version 1) are not reused.

Verified on Windows (Vulkan, AMD) in one game's battle: the stencil-only
shadow passes no longer show as dark shafts. Not verified: the order of
the operands of the less/greater functions, increment and decrement on
1- and 4-bit stencils, how the GE widens 1- and 4-bit alpha when it
compares, 4444 targets, frame interpolation's replays of stencil draws.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
